-- postpartum week 116: Finding room to enjoy cooking again, on your own terms (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   116,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Finding room to enjoy cooking again, on your own terms',
   '"Cooking doesn''t have to stay purely functional forever — and it''s also fine if some days it just is. Either way is a legitimate way to feed yourself."',
   'For much of the last couple of years, cooking for yourself may have taken a back seat to whatever was fastest or whatever the household needed. That was a reasonable way to get through it — and if the pace has started to ease, it''s also fine to notice whether you''d like a bit more of your own preference and enjoyment back in your own meals. There''s no requirement to become an enthusiastic home cook, and no timeline for wanting to. Some weeks, meals staying purely functional is exactly right. This week''s suggestions are simply here if you''d like a little more room for your own taste this time.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Haryanvi cooking (bajra khichdi, kadhi pakora, churma, sarson saag, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "For a long stretch, cooking may have been about getting something, anything, on the table — nothing wrong with that. If the pace of these last couple of years is starting to ease a little, this week is an invitation, not a requirement, to notice whether you''d enjoy putting a bit more of yourself back into a meal now and then.", "breakfast_a": "Bajra khichdi, pearl-millet khichdi (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kadhi pakora, gram-flour fritters in curd curry (1 cup, made with a measured 150g curd and about 30g dry besan across the kadhi base and 2 fritters) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kachri ki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Haryanvi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kachri ki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Bajra roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "One dish that''s just for you", "notes": "Family meals usually need to work for everyone at the table, which can mean your own preferences quietly come last. Cooking — or even just plating — one small dish the way you actually like it, without adjusting it for anyone else''s taste, is a small act worth allowing yourself now and then.", "breakfast_a": "Besan cheela, gram-flour crepe (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Sarson saag with a chana dal (1 cup saag, 1/2 cup dal) and a bajra roti (2 pieces)", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Haryanvi-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sarson saag", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Simplifying is still cooking for yourself", "notes": "Enjoying cooking again doesn''t mean every meal needs to be elaborate — a two-ingredient dal-and-rice you actually planned for yourself counts just as much as something more involved. The point is choosing it on purpose, not the number of steps it took.", "breakfast_a": "Churma, sweetened crushed-wheat crumble (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kadhi pakora, gram-flour fritters in curd curry (1 cup, made with a measured 150g curd and about 30g dry besan across the kadhi base and 2 fritters) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Haryanvi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Bajra roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Trying something new, without pressure", "notes": "If you feel like trying an unfamiliar recipe or ingredient this week, that''s a nice thing to have room for again — and if it doesn''t turn out the way you hoped, that''s genuinely fine too. There''s no deadline on rebuilding a sense of curiosity in the kitchen, and no obligation to have one at all.", "breakfast_a": "Malai kofta, paneer-and-potato dumplings in gravy (1 cup, 3-4 kofta) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana masala, Haryanvi-style chickpea curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an aloo-gobi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Haryanvi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an aloo-gobi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Sharing the load doesn''t mean losing your say", "notes": "If someone else in your household cooks some days, that''s a genuine help, not a loss of ownership over your own meals — you can still ask for what actually nourishes and appeals to you, rather than defaulting to whatever''s easiest for whoever''s cooking.", "breakfast_a": "Missi roti, spiced gram-flour roti (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Haryanvi-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Bajra roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one meal", "notes": "One rushed, thrown-together meal doesn''t undo an otherwise nourishing week, and one meal you genuinely enjoyed cooking doesn''t need repeating every day to count. What matters is the overall pattern of feeding yourself adequately — however each individual meal came together.", "breakfast_a": "Aloo paratha, spiced potato flatbread (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kadhi pakora, gram-flour fritters in curd curry (1 cup, made with a measured 150g curd and about 30g dry besan across the kadhi base and 2 fritters) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a gobi-matar sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Haryanvi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a gobi-matar sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a sarson saag", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Looking back at the week, was there a meal you actually enjoyed making — and is that worth remembering for a day you need a lift?", "breakfast_a": "Bajra khichdi, pearl-millet khichdi (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kadhi pakora, gram-flour fritters in curd curry (1 cup, made with a measured 150g curd and about 30g dry besan across the kadhi base and 2 fritters) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kachri ki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Haryanvi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kachri ki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Bajra roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Next week turns to something more practical: keeping your own food and water safe through the monsoon, not just everyone else''s.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 117: Your own food and water safety through the monsoon (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   117,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Your own food and water safety through the monsoon',
   '"If it''s monsoon season where you live, wet weather raises the odds of waterborne illness and spoiled food — a few consistent habits around water, greens and storage protect you just as much as anyone else in the house."',
   'If it''s monsoon season where you live, or you''re spending time somewhere that''s currently experiencing it, this is a good week to be a little more deliberate about food and water. If it isn''t monsoon season for you right now, these are still sound everyday habits — just less urgent this particular week. Wet weather changes the food-safety picture in a few specific ways: a higher chance of waterborne illness, greens more likely to carry soil-borne contamination, and damp storage conditions that let mould grow in dry staples. If your appetite dips in the heat and humidity, that''s common. Smaller, more frequent meals can nourish you just as well as fewer, larger ones, as long as your overall intake across the day stays adequate — but a marked or persistent drop in appetite, especially alongside other symptoms, is worth mentioning to your doctor rather than assuming it will pass.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Naga cooking (Naga dal, bamboo shoot curry, akhuni/fermented soybean chutney, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "If it''s monsoon season where you live, humid, wet weather makes it easier for bacteria and mould to grow in water, food and storage — a risk that applies to you too, not only to what you''re careful about for the rest of the household. A few consistent habits through the wetter months lower that risk without much extra effort.", "breakfast_a": "Naga dal, black-eyed bean curry with rice (1 cup dal, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked black-eyed beans, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Naga dal, black-eyed bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked black-eyed beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mustard greens sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Naga-style with king chili (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mustard greens sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Water and ice, especially when you''re eating out", "notes": "Waterborne illnesses like typhoid and hepatitis A become more common during the monsoon, often through contaminated water rather than food itself. When you''re eating outside the home, choose bottled or reliably treated water, skip ice unless you know its source, and be cautious with fresh juices and cut fruit sold on the street, where the water or ice used isn''t something you can verify. Worth knowing: Use commercially prepared bamboo shoots and follow the package instructions. Do not use raw or inadequately prepared fresh shoots.", "breakfast_a": "Akhuni chutney with rice, fermented soybean chutney (2 tbsp chutney, 1 cup rice) with a measured bowl of black-eyed beans (1/2 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Naga dal, black-eyed bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked black-eyed beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot curry", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Naga-style with bamboo shoot (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot curry", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Leafy greens during the monsoon", "notes": "Leafy greens are more likely to carry soil-borne bacteria or parasites picked up during heavy rain and flooding. Wash them thoroughly in clean running water, and lean toward cooking greens rather than eating them raw in salads during the wetter weeks of the season.", "breakfast_a": "Chousela roti with a Naga dal (2 pieces, 1 cup dal, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mustard greens sabzi, well cooked", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Naga-style with king chili (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mustard greens sabzi, well cooked", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If your appetite dips in humid weather", "notes": "A smaller appetite in hot, humid weather is common. Smaller, more frequent meals can nourish you just as well as three larger ones, as long as your overall intake across the day stays adequate — a freshly served portion and safely reheated leftovers are equally real meals. A marked or persistent drop in appetite, especially alongside other symptoms, is worth mentioning to your doctor rather than assuming it will pass.", "breakfast_a": "Rice with a pumpkin curry and a measured 1/2 cup cooked black-eyed beans (1 cup rice, 1 cup curry) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Naga dal, black-eyed bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked black-eyed beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Naga-style with king chili (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Checking stored grains and pulses", "notes": "Humidity makes it easier for mould to develop in grains, pulses and groundnuts stored at home, and some moulds produce toxins (aflatoxin among them) that aren''t destroyed by cooking. Store dry staples in airtight containers, check for a musty smell or visible mould before cooking, and discard anything questionable rather than trying to salvage it.", "breakfast_a": "Chousela roti with an akhuni chutney and a measured bowl of tofu bhurji (2 pieces, 2 tbsp chutney, 100g tofu) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana masala, chickpea curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squash sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Naga-style with king chili (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squash sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "If you''re unsure about something you ate or drank", "notes": "An uncertain food or water exposure is not a reason to blame yourself. Resume safer habits, but seek medical advice for bloody diarrhoea, persistent vomiting, dehydration, high fever or other concerning symptoms.", "breakfast_a": "Naga dal khichdi, rice-and-black-eyed-bean khichdi (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Naga dal, black-eyed bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked black-eyed beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry, Naga-style with king chili (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a squash sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Is there one water or storage habit from this week worth carrying through the rest of the season, even after the heaviest rain passes?", "breakfast_a": "Naga dal, black-eyed bean curry with rice (1 cup dal, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked black-eyed beans, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Naga dal, black-eyed bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked black-eyed beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mustard greens sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Naga-style with king chili (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mustard greens sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Next week is a practical one: planning and packing your own meals for the days you''re out of the house, not just everyone else''s.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
