-- postpartum week 118: Planning and packing your own meals for days away from home (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   118,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Planning and packing your own meals for days away from home',
   '"When you''re planning what everyone else eats on a busy day, your own meal is often the one that gets skipped — a little planning ahead keeps it on the list too."',
   'Days away from home — work, errands, appointments — usually get planned around everyone else''s meals, with your own left to whatever''s convenient in the moment, or skipped. A little planning changes that: a handful of dishes that travel well, a container strategy, and a stocked backup list at home make it realistic to feed yourself properly on days you''re not eating at your own table. This is as much a food-safety topic as a planning one — food carried with you needs the same care around temperature and timing as food left out at home.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Ladakhi cooking (thukpa, skyu, chutagi, tingmo, khambir, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "On a day you''re out of the house — work, errands, an appointment — it''s easy to plan carefully for what everyone else will eat and leave your own meal to chance. A packed lunch or a stocked snack for yourself deserves the same five minutes of planning as anyone else''s.", "breakfast_a": "Barley porridge, ngamphe-style with dried apricots (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Thukpa, noodle-and-vegetable soup (one serving prepared with 1 cup cooked wheat noodles, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils or beans, and 1 cup vegetables, measured after cooking, plus broth as preferred — finished volume varies) with a khambir, Ladakhi bread (1 piece)", "lunch_b": "Chicken thukpa, noodle-and-chicken soup (one serving prepared with 1 cup cooked wheat noodles, 100g cooked chicken, and 1 cup vegetables, measured after cooking, plus broth as preferred — finished volume varies) with a khambir, Ladakhi bread (1 piece)", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Dishes that travel and reheat well", "notes": "Not every meal packs and reheats equally well. A dal or bean curry, a dry vegetable sabzi, a grain like rice or barley, and a roti or khambir all hold their safety and quality well when kept at a proper temperature — cold at 4°C or below, or hot at 60°C or above — for as long as you''ll be carrying them, and reheat cleanly, which makes them a dependable starting point for your own packed meals.", "breakfast_a": "Tingmo, steamed bread with a chana dal (2 pieces, 1 cup dal, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chutagi, pasta-and-dal stew (one serving prepared with 1 cup cooked wheat pasta shapes, a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal, and 1 cup vegetables, measured after cooking, plus broth as preferred — finished volume varies) with a khambir, Ladakhi bread (1 piece)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Ladakhi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "The two-hour rule for food you''re carrying", "notes": "The same two-hour rule that applies to food left out at home applies to whatever you''re carrying with you. A dish that''s dependably safe to eat at home isn''t automatically safe several hours later in a bag — plan the container and its temperature, not just the meal. Worth knowing: Perishable packed food shouldn''t sit at room temperature for more than two hours — or one hour if the weather is hot (above about 32°C). Keep cold food at 4°C or below, in an insulated bag packed with enough frozen ice packs to hold that temperature. Keep hot food at 60°C or above, in a preheated insulated flask — rinse it with boiling water and let it stand a few minutes, then empty it just before filling it with the hot food. An ordinary, non-insulated container won''t hold either temperature for long.", "breakfast_a": "Khambir with a moong dal (2 pieces, 1 cup dal, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Skyu, wheat-dumpling-and-vegetable stew (one serving prepared with 1 cup cooked wheat dumplings, a measured 1/2 cup cooked beans, and 1 cup vegetables, measured after cooking, plus broth as preferred — finished volume varies) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Ladakhi-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a turnip sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "A stocked \"grab and go\" list at home", "notes": "Some days there simply isn''t time to pack a full meal, and that''s fine — keeping a few ready things on hand (boiled eggs, a fruit, a small curd cup, roasted nuts) means you still have something worth eating on the way out, rather than skipping a meal entirely.", "breakfast_a": "Skyu, wheat-dumpling-and-vegetable stew (one serving prepared with 1 cup cooked wheat dumplings, a measured 1/2 cup cooked beans, and 1 cup vegetables, measured after cooking, plus broth as preferred — finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Thukpa, noodle-and-vegetable soup (one serving prepared with 1 cup cooked wheat noodles, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils or beans, and 1 cup vegetables, measured after cooking, plus broth as preferred — finished volume varies) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Chicken thukpa, noodle-and-chicken soup (one serving prepared with 1 cup cooked wheat noodles, 100g cooked chicken, and 1 cup vegetables, measured after cooking, plus broth as preferred — finished volume varies) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Cooking once, eating from it for a few days", "notes": "A larger batch of dal, a grain and a vegetable sabzi cooked once can cover your own packed meals for a few days, not just one. Divide it into small, shallow containers right after cooking, while it''s still hot, rather than leaving the whole pot to cool on the counter first — shallow portions cool faster and more evenly, which is what keeps them safe. Refrigerate promptly once they''ve stopped steaming, and date the containers. Most cooked dal, grain and vegetable dishes keep well refrigerated for 3-4 days; cooked rice is more time-sensitive — refrigerate it within about an hour of cooking and use it within 24 hours, or freeze it for later.", "breakfast_a": "Chutagi, pasta-and-dal stew (one serving prepared with 1 cup cooked wheat pasta shapes, a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal, and 1 cup vegetables, measured after cooking, plus broth as preferred — finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Skyu, wheat-dumpling-and-vegetable stew (one serving prepared with 1 cup cooked wheat dumplings, a measured 1/2 cup cooked beans, and 1 cup vegetables, measured after cooking, plus broth as preferred — finished volume varies) with a khambir, Ladakhi bread (1 piece)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Ladakhi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a turnip sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Giving your own meals the same planning care", "notes": "You likely already plan carefully around what your family eats when you''re all out together — the same planning, applied to your own meals on the days you''re out alone, is just as worthwhile. It''s not extra effort so much as effort redirected to include yourself.", "breakfast_a": "Barley porridge, ngamphe-style with walnuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chutagi, pasta-and-dal stew (one serving prepared with 1 cup cooked wheat pasta shapes, a measured 1/2 cup cooked dal, and 1 cup vegetables, measured after cooking, plus broth as preferred — finished volume varies) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Ladakhi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squash sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a turnip sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Is there one dish from this week worth keeping on hand specifically because it travels well?", "breakfast_a": "Barley porridge, ngamphe-style with dried apricots (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Thukpa, noodle-and-vegetable soup (one serving prepared with 1 cup cooked wheat noodles, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils or beans, and 1 cup vegetables, measured after cooking, plus broth as preferred — finished volume varies) with a khambir, Ladakhi bread (1 piece)", "lunch_b": "Chicken thukpa, noodle-and-chicken soup (one serving prepared with 1 cup cooked wheat noodles, 100g cooked chicken, and 1 cup vegetables, measured after cooking, plus broth as preferred — finished volume varies) with a khambir, Ladakhi bread (1 piece)", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Next week looks at a mineral that gets less attention than it deserves: potassium, and what it actually does for you.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 119: Potassium, a mineral worth knowing about beyond bananas (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   119,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Potassium, a mineral worth knowing about beyond bananas',
   '"Potassium works alongside sodium in your fluid balance, nerve and muscle function, and blood pressure — and it comes from far more everyday foods than the one fruit it''s usually associated with."',
   'Potassium supports fluid balance, nerve signalling, muscle function and — alongside a lower-sodium diet — healthy blood pressure. It comes from a wide range of everyday foods: potatoes with the skin on, leafy greens, curd, coconut water, beans, lentils, citrus fruit and, yes, bananas too. Healthy kidneys usually regulate potassium from food. Kidney disease and certain medicines can change this, so food, drinks, supplements and potassium-containing salt substitutes may all need individual guidance. Some common medicines — including ACE inhibitors, ARBs and potassium-sparing diuretics — can raise blood potassium; if you take one of these, ask your prescriber whether your potassium intake needs any particular attention, rather than changing your medicine on your own. This is a nutrient worth understanding through diet, not one to add as a supplement without medical guidance.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you have kidney disease: follow your renal team''s specific guidance on potassium — your needs may be lower than the general pattern in this series, and some of this week''s foods may need adjusting or limiting for you. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Khasi cooking (jadoh, tungrymbai/fermented soybean curry, pumpkin stew, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Potassium doesn''t get talked about as often as sodium, but it plays a comparable role — supporting fluid balance, nerve signalling, muscle function and blood pressure. It''s genuinely useful, but that doesn''t mean more is automatically better, especially if you take a supplement without medical guidance.", "breakfast_a": "Jadoh-style rice, a vegetarian adaptation of Khasi jadoh made with a measured bean component rather than meat or meat stock (1 cup rice with 1/2 cup cooked black-eyed beans) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Tungrymbai, fermented soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked soybeans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin stew", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Khasi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin stew", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Food sources beyond bananas", "notes": "Bananas are a well-known potassium source, but potatoes with the skin on, spinach and other leafy greens, curd, coconut water, beans and lentils, and oranges or other citrus all contribute meaningfully too. Eating a varied diet across the week covers this more reliably than relying on any single food.", "breakfast_a": "Pumpkin stew with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked black-eyed beans (1 cup stew, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Tungrymbai, fermented soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked soybeans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Khasi-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "If you have kidney disease", "notes": "Most of the general potassium guidance in this series assumes typical kidney function. If you have kidney disease, that guidance may not apply to you the same way — your care team''s individual advice always takes priority over anything general written here. Worth knowing: With reduced kidney function, potassium can build up in your blood rather than being cleared normally, and too much can affect your heart rhythm. If you have kidney disease, potassium needs may be LOWER than the general guidance in this series suggests, not higher — follow your renal team''s specific advice on which foods and how much potassium suit you.", "breakfast_a": "Rice with tungrymbai, fermented soybean curry (1 cup rice, 1/2 cup curry, includes a measured 1/4 cup cooked soybeans) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squash sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Khasi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squash sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Why a potassium supplement isn''t something to self-start", "notes": "A potassium supplement taken without medical supervision can push your blood levels too high and affect your heart rhythm — this isn''t a nutrient to add on your own just because it sounds beneficial. Certain medicines, including ACE inhibitors, ARBs and potassium-sparing diuretics, can also raise blood potassium — if you take one of these, check with your prescriber before changing your potassium intake, rather than adjusting your medicine yourself. If a clinician has a specific reason to recommend a supplement for you, that''s different from choosing to start one on your own.", "breakfast_a": "Tungrymbai on toast, fermented soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked soybeans, 2 slices toast) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Tungrymbai, fermented soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked soybeans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Khasi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Potassium and blood pressure", "notes": "Potassium works alongside a lower-sodium diet to support healthy blood pressure — the two aren''t separate strategies so much as two sides of the same one. This complements the salt-and-sugar habits covered earlier in this series rather than replacing them; neither on its own is a complete approach to blood pressure.", "breakfast_a": "Bean-and-vegetable salad, a vegetarian adaptation of Khasi dohkhlieh made with a measured bean component rather than meat (1 cup salad, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana masala, chickpea curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an aloo sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Khasi-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an aloo sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one food", "notes": "No single potassium-rich food is doing all the work on its own — the pattern across the week, a mix of vegetables, fruit, dairy, beans and grains, is what actually matters. There''s no need to track this nutrient closely if your overall diet is reasonably varied and your kidney function and medicines are unremarkable — unless your care team has advised otherwise.", "breakfast_a": "Squash sabzi with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked black-eyed beans (1 cup sabzi, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Tungrymbai, fermented soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked soybeans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Khasi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a squash sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Beyond bananas, is there a potassium-rich food from this week you''d actually keep eating regularly?", "breakfast_a": "Jadoh-style rice, a vegetarian adaptation of Khasi jadoh made with a measured bean component rather than meat or meat stock (1 cup rice with 1/2 cup cooked black-eyed beans) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Tungrymbai, fermented soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked soybeans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin stew", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Khasi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin stew", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Next week closes this run with something many mothers quietly navigate: what to do if dairy doesn''t sit right with you anymore.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 120: If dairy doesn't sit right anymore — lactose intolerance and calcium from other sources (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   120,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'If dairy doesn''t sit right anymore — lactose intolerance and calcium from other sources',
   '"Reduced dairy tolerance is common in adults and can develop or worsen with age — it''s a different thing from a milk allergy, and it doesn''t have to mean giving up on calcium."',
   'Lactose tolerance can decline with age, and it''s especially common in South Asian adults — noticing bloating, gas or discomfort after dairy at some point in adulthood is common. That said, bloating alone doesn''t establish lactose intolerance; persistent or unexplained digestive symptoms are worth raising with your doctor rather than self-diagnosing. Lactose intolerance is also a different thing entirely from a milk protein allergy, which is an immune reaction rather than a digestive one, and the two need different handling. This week''s meals use lactose-free milk, curd and paneer throughout, so reduced tolerance doesn''t have to mean losing your dependable calcium sources — and if dairy in any form genuinely doesn''t work for you, fortified or calcium-set alternatives, checked on the label, can take over that role instead. If you tolerate ordinary dairy without any discomfort, you''re welcome to use it instead — lactose-free products are an option here, not a requirement.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you have a diagnosed milk protein allergy rather than lactose intolerance: lactose-free dairy is not safe for you — it still contains the allergenic proteins. Use a genuinely dairy-free, calcium-fortified alternative instead, and follow your own allergy plan. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Lactose-free curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml), the same as ordinary dairy; a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — this week, lactose-free curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy altogether, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Kutchi cooking (dabeli, Kutchi kadhi, khichdi, bajra rotla, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "If milk, curd or other dairy has started leaving you bloated, gassy or uncomfortable, that''s common — lactose tolerance can decline with age, and it''s especially common among South Asian adults. Bloating on its own doesn''t confirm lactose intolerance, though; if the discomfort is new, persistent or unexplained, or comes with other symptoms, it''s worth mentioning to your doctor rather than assuming it''s lactose. This week''s meals use lactose-free milk and curd throughout, so the calcium habit doesn''t have to stop just because ordinary dairy doesn''t agree with you right now.", "breakfast_a": "Dabeli, spiced-potato bun (1 bun) with a small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of lactose-free milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kutchi kadhi, curd-based curry (1 cup, includes a measured 200g lactose-free curd and 2 tbsp besan) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lilva sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Kutchi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lilva sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of lactose-free milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Bajra rotla (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry, made with lactose-free paneer (100g) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Lactose intolerance and a milk allergy are different things", "notes": "Lactose intolerance means your body struggles to digest the sugar in milk — uncomfortable, but not dangerous, and lactose-free dairy usually solves it because the lactose itself has been broken down. A true milk protein allergy is a different, immune-system reaction to the proteins in dairy, and lactose-free products still contain those proteins — they are never a safe substitute for an actual milk allergy. If you''re not sure which one you have, a doctor can help you tell them apart.", "breakfast_a": "Khichdi, Kutchi-style rice-and-dal khichdi (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal) with a small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of lactose-free milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kutchi kadhi, curd-based curry (1 cup, includes a measured 200g lactose-free curd and 2 tbsp besan) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Kutchi-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of lactose-free milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g)", "dinner_a": "Bajra rotla (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Fermented dairy is sometimes easier to tolerate", "notes": "Curd and yoghurt contain live cultures that break down some of the lactose during fermentation, which means some people who react to milk tolerate curd reasonably well. It varies from person to person, so this is worth noticing for yourself rather than assuming it''ll work the same way it does for someone else.", "breakfast_a": "Sev khamani, steamed-and-crumbled gram-flour cake (1 cup) with a small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of lactose-free milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana masala, chickpea curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sev tameta nu shaak", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Kutchi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sev tameta nu shaak", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of lactose-free milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry, made with lactose-free paneer (100g) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Reading labels on lactose-free products", "notes": "\"Lactose-free\" and \"non-dairy\" aren''t the same claim — a lactose-free product is still dairy, made from milk with the lactose broken down or removed. A \"non-dairy\" label isn''t a reliable guarantee that a product contains no milk at all — depending on where you live, some products labelled non-dairy can still legally contain milk-derived ingredients such as caseinates. If a milk allergy is involved, check the full ingredients list and allergen declaration rather than relying on the front-of-pack claim, and follow your own allergy plan. If you''re managing lactose intolerance specifically, checking the label still matters, so you know which kind of product you''re actually choosing.", "breakfast_a": "Bajra rotla with a chana dal (2 pieces, 1 cup dal, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with a small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of lactose-free milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kutchi kadhi, curd-based curry (1 cup, includes a measured 200g lactose-free curd and 2 tbsp besan) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a gawar sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Kutchi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a gawar sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of lactose-free milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Bajra rotla (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If dairy genuinely doesn''t work for you at all", "notes": "If lactose-free dairy still doesn''t agree with you, or you''d rather avoid dairy altogether: swap milk for a calcium-fortified plant milk, curd for a calcium-fortified plant-based yoghurt, and paneer for calcium-set tofu — check the label on each for actual calcium content, since not all plant products are fortified, and check protein content too, since it varies a lot between brands and isn''t automatically equivalent to dairy''s. Kadhi is trickier, since its base is curd itself — a besan-and-plant-yoghurt version, or a plain besan-and-vegetable curry without the curd base, are reasonable substitutes if a curd-based kadhi doesn''t work for you. If you replace curd-based kadhi with a dairy-free vegetable-and-besan curry, keep a measured calcium source elsewhere in that meal or day, such as calcium-set tofu or a calcium-fortified product — check protein content too. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding, and a food choice alone doesn''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.", "breakfast_a": "Handvo, savoury rice-and-lentil cake (1 cup) with a small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of lactose-free milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a papeta nu shaak", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Kutchi-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a papeta nu shaak", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of calcium-fortified soy milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry, made with lactose-free paneer (100g) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one uncomfortable meal", "notes": "If you have lactose intolerance and ordinary dairy catches you out once — at someone else''s house, or before you''d checked a label — that''s not a setback worth dwelling on; getting back to what works for you is what matters, not any single meal. If you have a diagnosed milk protein allergy, accidental exposure is different and isn''t something to simply wait out — follow your own allergy action plan, and seek medical care for any signs of an allergic reaction.", "breakfast_a": "Dhokla, steamed gram-flour cake (4 pieces) with a small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of lactose-free milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kutchi kadhi, curd-based curry (1 cup, includes a measured 200g lactose-free curd and 2 tbsp besan) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ringna nu shaak", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Kutchi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ringna nu shaak", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of lactose-free milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a sev tameta nu shaak", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Did lactose-free dairy work for you this week, or is a fully dairy-free calcium source worth exploring further with your doctor?", "breakfast_a": "Dabeli, spiced-potato bun (1 bun) with a small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of lactose-free milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kutchi kadhi, curd-based curry (1 cup, includes a measured 200g lactose-free curd and 2 tbsp besan) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lilva sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Kutchi-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lilva sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of lactose-free milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Bajra rotla (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry, made with lactose-free paneer (100g) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of lactose-free curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'This closes the current run of weekly themes. Whatever comes next in this series, the idea underneath all of it stays the same: your own nourishment deserves the same attention as everyone else''s at the table.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 121: Folate in your everyday plate, not just for planning a pregnancy (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   121,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Folate in your everyday plate, not just for planning a pregnancy',
   '"Folate keeps your body making new red blood cells and repairing tissue day after day — a role that matters whether or not another pregnancy is anywhere in your thinking."',
   'Folate (vitamin B9) supports the ongoing production of red blood cells and the everyday repair of tissue in your body — a role that continues long after pregnancy and birth, and one that breastfeeding raises the requirement for. It''s a different topic from the pre-conception folic acid guidance covered earlier in this series (Week 50): that''s specifically about the run-up to conception and the early weeks of pregnancy, while this week is about the everyday dietary role folate plays regardless of your plans. Leafy greens, legumes, citrus fruit and fortified grains are all dependable everyday sources, and food folate doesn''t carry the same upper-limit concerns that high-dose supplements can — these foods help contribute to your needs across a varied week.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you''ve been told you''re anaemic: ask your care team which nutrient your results point to — anaemia has multiple possible causes, and the right supplement depends on which one applies to you. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Mizo cooking (bai, bekang/fermented soybean, sawhchiar, chhum han, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Folate needs don''t disappear once a pregnancy ends. Your body uses it continuously for red blood cell production and everyday tissue repair, and breastfeeding raises that requirement further. Leafy greens, legumes, citrus fruit and fortified grains are all dependable everyday sources.", "breakfast_a": "Bai, mixed vegetable stew with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked beans (1 cup stew, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with tomato and onion, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Bekang, fermented soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked soybeans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Mizo-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Food folate and supplement folic acid aren''t quite the same thing", "notes": "Folate occurring naturally in food and folic acid in supplements or fortified foods both count toward your intake, but they''re handled somewhat differently by the body — food folate doesn''t carry the same upper-limit concerns that high-dose supplemental folic acid can. Getting a good share from food, rather than supplements alone, is a reasonable everyday approach.", "breakfast_a": "Chhum han, boiled mixed vegetables with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked beans (1 cup vegetables, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with a vegetable poha, flattened rice with peas and carrots (1 cup) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Bekang, fermented soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked soybeans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "River fish curry, Mizo-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "If you''re anaemic or your haemoglobin is low", "notes": "If a blood test has flagged low haemoglobin, folate is one of several nutrients that could be involved. Ask your care team what your specific results point to rather than assuming folate is the answer and supplementing on your own. Worth knowing: Anaemia has several possible causes. Which one applies to you can only be determined by assessment — a blood test guides which nutrient, if any, actually needs addressing; treating a guess with a supplement isn''t a substitute for that.", "breakfast_a": "Sawhchiar, rice-and-vegetable porridge with carrots (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked beans, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Bekang, fermented soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked soybeans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Mizo-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer tikka, grilled spiced paneer skewers with capsicum and onion (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a cucumber-tomato salad", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If another pregnancy might be ahead", "notes": "This is different from the everyday folate covered elsewhere this week. If pregnancy is anywhere in your thinking, standard guidance is to start a daily 400 microgram folic acid supplement now, before trying — not to wait for a positive test. This guidance continues into the early weeks of pregnancy too, not just the run-up to conceiving; keep taking it once you know you''re pregnant, until your care team advises otherwise. Raise it with your care team if you haven''t already; this is worth acting on early rather than treating as a food-only question.", "breakfast_a": "Bai with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked beans (1 cup stew, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Mizo-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Leafy greens, legumes and citrus — everyday sources", "notes": "Spinach and other leafy greens, rajma and other beans, lentils, oranges and other citrus fruit, and fortified grains all contribute meaningfully to folate intake. None of them needs to be eaten daily on its own — these foods help contribute to your needs across a varied week.", "breakfast_a": "Chhum han with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked beans (1 cup vegetables, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable uttapam, rice-and-lentil batter pancake with mixed vegetables (2 eggs, 2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana masala, chickpea curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an aloo-palak sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Mizo-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and an aloo-palak sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a bottle-gourd sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one meal", "notes": "No single folate-rich food is carrying the whole week on its own — beans, greens, citrus and fortified grains showing up across your meals, rather than in one deliberate dish, is what actually adds up. There''s no need to track this nutrient meal by meal if your overall diet is reasonably varied.", "breakfast_a": "Sawhchiar, rice-and-vegetable porridge (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked beans, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Bekang, fermented soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked soybeans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans-and-carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Mizo-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans-and-carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut melon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg bhurji-stuffed roti wrap, spiced scrambled eggs rolled in chapati with onion and tomato (2 eggs, 2 chapati) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Which everyday folate source from this week — a green, a bean, a citrus fruit — would you actually keep eating regularly?", "breakfast_a": "Bai, mixed vegetable stew with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked beans (1 cup stew, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with tomato and onion, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Bekang, fermented soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked soybeans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Mizo-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Next week looks at something most households navigate without much thought: your own drinking water, and what "safe" actually means.')
