-- postpartum week 122: Understanding your own drinking water — RO purifiers, TDS, and what "safe" means (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   122,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Understanding your own drinking water — RO purifiers, TDS, and what "safe" means',
   '"A TDS number on a purifier display tells you about dissolved minerals, not about germs — knowing what it does and doesn''t measure helps you make sense of the reading rather than either ignoring it or over-worrying about it."',
   'Most households manage drinking water through some kind of purifier without thinking much about what it actually does. A TDS reading measures dissolved minerals and salts — a high or low number tells you about taste and mineral content, not about bacteria or viruses, and it cannot by itself establish that water is safe to drink. RO purification removes dissolved solids, including beneficial minerals, and very low TDS isn''t automatically better; the World Health Organization hasn''t set a health-based guideline value for TDS, and India''s drinking water standard (IS 10500) treats it mainly as an acceptability and taste parameter rather than a safety threshold. RO membranes can remove many microorganisms as well as dissolved minerals, but real-world performance depends on the specific system, how well it''s maintained, and safe storage afterward — no single stage or technology is an unconditional guarantee against every contaminant. Knowing what your specific purifier is certified to remove, for your specific source water, matters more than a single brand claim or category label.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you''re on a low-sodium or fluid-restricted plan for a kidney or heart condition: ask your care team whether your household water''s mineral content is something they want you to factor in — this varies by individual situation and isn''t something to judge from a TDS number alone. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Malvani/Konkan coastal Maharashtrian cooking (sanna, amboli, sol kadi, usal, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "TDS stands for total dissolved solids — a measure of minerals and salts dissolved in water, not a measure of microbial safety. A high or low TDS reading tells you something about mineral content and taste, but it doesn''t by itself tell you whether the water is free of bacteria or viruses. The two are different questions.", "breakfast_a": "Sanna, steamed rice cakes with coconut chutney (3 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with tomato and onion, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Usal, sprouted moth bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked moth beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Malvani chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What RO purification actually does", "notes": "Reverse osmosis (RO) purification removes dissolved salts and many contaminants, but it strips out beneficial minerals along with the unwanted ones — which is part of why RO water often reads a very low TDS. Very low TDS isn''t automatically \"more pure\" or better for you: the World Health Organization hasn''t set a health-based guideline value for TDS, and India''s drinking water standard (IS 10500) treats it mainly as an acceptability and taste parameter, not a safety threshold. A TDS number, high or low, doesn''t by itself establish that water is microbially or chemically safe.", "breakfast_a": "Amboli, rice-and-coconut pancake with coconut chutney (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette with tomato and onion (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Usal, sprouted moth bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked moth beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Malvani fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of sol kadi, kokum-and-coconut milk drink (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable stir-fry, sautéed paneer with mixed vegetables (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a cucumber raita", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Matching your purifier to your actual water source", "notes": "The right purification approach depends on what''s actually present in your source water, not on how it looks, tastes or feels — hardness or a strong taste don''t by themselves tell you what treatment is needed, and their absence doesn''t mean the water is safe either. A water-testing service can identify what''s actually in your water; choose a purifier independently verified to remove those specific contaminants, rather than picking one based on taste, appearance, or marketing claims.", "breakfast_a": "Sanna with a sukka sabzi (3 pieces, 1 cup sabzi) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable dalia, broken wheat porridge with carrots and peas (2 eggs stirred in, 1 cup dalia) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Chana masala, chickpea curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squash sabzi", "lunch_b": "Malvani chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squash sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot-and-peas sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Mineral content and microbial safety are different questions", "notes": "A purifier''s real-world performance against minerals and against microbes both depend on its specific components, upkeep and safe storage afterward — not simply on which broad category (RO, UV, ultrafiltration) it falls into. Checking what your specific system is certified to remove for your specific source water, and keeping it serviced, matters more than any single brand claim. Worth knowing: RO membranes can remove many microorganisms, including bacteria and most viruses, when the system is intact and properly maintained — this isn''t a job reserved exclusively for a UV or ultrafiltration stage. But no single stage or technology is an unconditional guarantee against every possible contaminant: actual performance depends on the specific system, how well it''s maintained, and how the water is stored afterward. If your municipal supply is known to be microbially unsafe, check what your specific purifier is certified to remove, or use boiling as a fallback.", "breakfast_a": "Amboli with a sukka sabzi (2 pieces, 1 cup sabzi) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Usal, sprouted moth bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked moth beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Malvani chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Filters and membranes need routine servicing", "notes": "A purifier''s filter cartridges and RO membrane lose effectiveness over time and need replacing on the schedule the manufacturer specifies, not just when the water starts tasting or looking different — by the time you notice a change, the filter may have been underperforming for a while. Keeping a simple record of when each part was last changed is a small habit that keeps the whole system doing what it''s meant to.", "breakfast_a": "Sanna with coconut chutney (3 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a papeta sabzi", "lunch_b": "Bombil fry, Bombay duck fish fry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a papeta sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-vegetable biryani-style rice, spiced rice with boiled eggs and mixed vegetables (2 eggs, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "If you drank water from an uncertain source", "notes": "If you drank water from an uncertain source, there is no need to blame yourself. Use reliably safe water going forward. Seek medical advice if you develop persistent vomiting, bloody diarrhoea, fever or signs of dehydration.", "breakfast_a": "Sanna, steamed rice cakes with a sukka sabzi (3 pieces, 1 cup sabzi) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with a vegetable-and-cheese toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Usal, sprouted moth bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked moth beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge-gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Malvani chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge-gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a squash sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a cabbage-and-carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Do you know which purification stages your own household purifier actually has, and when the filters were last changed?", "breakfast_a": "Sanna, steamed rice cakes with coconut chutney (3 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with tomato and onion, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Usal, sprouted moth bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked moth beans) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Malvani chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
