-- postpartum week 123: Vitamin B6 — a steady everyday nutrient, not a mood supplement to self-dose (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   123,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Vitamin B6 — a steady everyday nutrient, not a mood supplement to self-dose',
   '"B6 supports protein metabolism, red blood cell formation and neurotransmitter production day to day — everyday foods help contribute to your needs, and supplemental B6 carries its own risk rather than being an automatic extra."',
   'Vitamin B6 supports protein metabolism, red blood cell formation and the everyday production of neurotransmitters. Most people get enough through ordinary meals — bananas, potatoes, legumes, whole grains, poultry and fish are all dependable sources, and none of them needs to appear in every meal to add up across a varied week. B6 is sometimes marketed as a standalone supplement for mood or premenstrual symptoms, but supplemental B6 can cause nerve damage — including when several products (a multivitamin, a magnesium supplement, a B-complex) each contribute some B6 and add up without you realising it. Cases have been reported at lower supplemental doses too, particularly when products are combined; a minimum dose or duration associated with this reaction has not been established. Food sources don''t carry this risk — it''s specifically a supplement concern, and a reason to check labels and your total intake with your care team rather than a reason to be wary of B6-rich foods.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you''re on isoniazid or another medicine known to affect B6 levels: your prescriber may already have this factored into your treatment plan — confirm with them rather than adding your own B6 supplement on top of what''s prescribed. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Udupi / coastal Karnataka vegetarian cooking (idli, dosa, sambar, rasam, upma, palya, kosambari, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Vitamin B6 supports your body''s protein metabolism, red blood cell production and the everyday synthesis of neurotransmitters. It''s a nutrient most people get enough of through ordinary meals, without needing a dedicated supplement or a specially engineered dish.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (4 pieces) with sambar and coconut chutney (1 cup sambar) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with tomato and onion, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Huli, lentil-and-vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a palya", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, coastal Karnataka-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a palya", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Everyday food sources", "notes": "Bananas, potatoes, chickpeas and other legumes, poultry, fish and fortified cereals are all dependable B6 sources. None of them needs to appear in every meal — spread across a varied week, they help contribute to your needs without any special effort.", "breakfast_a": "Masala dosa with a potato palya and coconut chutney (1 dosa, 1/2 cup potato filling) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable khichdi, rice-and-moong dal breakfast dish with mixed vegetables (2 eggs stirred in, 1 cup khichdi) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Chana huli, chickpea curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a palya", "lunch_b": "Mangalorean-style fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a palya", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "If you''re taking a B6-containing supplement", "notes": "B6 is sometimes marketed as a standalone supplement for mood or premenstrual symptoms. Cases of nerve damage have been reported at lower supplemental doses too, particularly when products are combined — a minimum dose or duration associated with this reaction has not been established. Checking your total B6 intake from all supplements against your care team''s advice is worth doing, whatever the individual doses. Worth knowing: Supplemental B6 can cause nerve damage. Cases have been reported at lower supplemental doses too, particularly when products are combined — check multivitamin, magnesium and B-complex labels. If tingling, burning or numbness develops, stop non-prescribed B6-containing supplements and seek medical advice promptly. If B6 is prescribed as part of treatment, contact your prescriber promptly for instructions. This isn''t a reason to avoid B6-rich foods — it''s specifically about supplemental B6.", "breakfast_a": "Rava upma with vegetables (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Huli, lentil-and-vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans palya", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, coastal Karnataka-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans palya", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-vegetable stew, boiled eggs simmered with mixed vegetables in a light gravy (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you take isoniazid or certain other medicines", "notes": "A small number of medicines, including isoniazid (used for tuberculosis treatment) and some others, are known to lower the body''s B6 levels over time. If you''re on one of these, your prescriber may already have this covered with a specific B6 dose as part of your treatment plan — this is worth confirming with them rather than adding your own supplement on top of what''s prescribed.", "breakfast_a": "Idli with sambar and coconut chutney (4 pieces, 1 cup sambar) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-mushroom bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs with mushrooms and onion (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a palya", "lunch_b": "Mangalorean-style prawn curry (100g prawns) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a palya", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Whole grains and legumes, day to day", "notes": "Whole grains, legumes and a modest variety of vegetables and fruit across the week form a dependable base for B6, alongside poultry and fish for those who eat them. These foods help contribute to your B6 needs without needing a dedicated tracking effort.", "breakfast_a": "Avalakki, spiced flattened rice with peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Huli, lentil-and-vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kosambari", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, coastal Karnataka-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a kosambari", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-methi curry, paneer with fenugreek leaves (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a beetroot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one meal", "notes": "No single B6-rich food is doing all the work on its own — bananas, potatoes, legumes, grains and, for those who eat them, poultry and fish, showing up across the week is what actually matters. There''s no need to track this nutrient meal by meal if your overall diet is reasonably varied.", "breakfast_a": "Rava upma with vegetables and roasted peanuts (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Huli, lentil-and-vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage palya", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, coastal Karnataka-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage palya", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a squash sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a cabbage-and-carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Which everyday B6 source from this week — a banana, a legume dish, a grain — would you actually keep eating regularly?", "breakfast_a": "Idli (4 pieces) with sambar and coconut chutney (1 cup sambar) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with tomato and onion, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Huli, lentil-and-vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a palya", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, coastal Karnataka-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a palya", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Next week takes a step back to look at your own plate as a whole — putting together everything this series has covered about balance, rather than one nutrient at a time.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
