-- postpartum week 124: Putting it together — your own balanced plate, revisited (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   124,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Putting it together — your own balanced plate, revisited',
   '"A balanced plate isn''t about hitting perfect proportions at every single meal — it''s a dependable protein source, a dependable calcium source, a grain, and vegetables or fruit, showing up across your day."',
   'This series has repeated the same handful of building blocks since its earliest weeks: a dependable protein source, a dependable calcium source, a grain, and vegetables or fruit, spread across the day rather than crammed into one meal. This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient — it''s a practical pause to see how those pieces fit together in an ordinary day''s eating. None of the individual pieces needs to be perfect at every meal. Protein, calcium, grains and produce adding up across several days is what actually matters, and there''s more than one valid way to cover each one. A balanced plate like this is about giving your body what it needs to function well — enough energy for your day, the building blocks for strength and recovery — rather than being framed around a weight outcome. If weight is something you''re thinking about, that''s a personal choice to raise with your own care team; this series isn''t offering a weight-management plan, and eating adequately here isn''t in tension with whatever goals you''re working toward.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Anglo-Indian cooking (kedgeree, dalcha, railway curry, country captain, ball curry, and similar). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "This series has repeated the same handful of building blocks since Week 43: a dependable protein source, a dependable calcium source, a grain, and vegetables or fruit. None of that has changed — this week is simply about seeing how those pieces fit together across an ordinary day, rather than learning something new.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable kedgeree, rice-and-lentil breakfast dish (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with tomato and onion, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalcha, dal-and-vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Country captain chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Protein — several valid sources, not one right answer", "notes": "Dal and other legumes, paneer, tofu, eggs, meat and fish are all valid protein sources — different in nutrient profile and not interchangeable at the same portion size, but each a legitimate way to cover this building block. There''s no single \"correct\" protein for every meal, only what suits your appetite, budget and dietary choices that day.", "breakfast_a": "Devilled potato toast, spiced mashed potato on toast (2 slices) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette with tomato and onion (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana dalcha, chickpea-and-vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Railway mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-vegetable cutlets, spiced patties with mashed vegetables and egg (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a tomato-onion salad", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Calcium — the two-a-day habit this series keeps returning to", "notes": "The calcium habit repeated across this series — curd, milk or paneer at more than one meal, or a measured fortified alternative if you avoid dairy — is the same one that applies here. It''s worth keeping even in a week that isn''t specifically about calcium, since it''s easy to let slip once the topic isn''t front and centre.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable kedgeree with peas (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum paratha, stuffed flatbread with spiced egg and capsicum filling (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Moong dalcha, moong dal-and-vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squash sabzi", "lunch_b": "Country captain chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squash sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot-and-peas sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Vegetables and fruit — variety, not one \"superfood\"", "notes": "No single vegetable or fruit needs to carry the whole week — different colours and types across your meals cover a broader range of nutrients than repeating the same one, however nutritious it is on its own. Whatever''s seasonal, affordable and easy to prepare is a completely reasonable way to choose.", "breakfast_a": "Bread (2 slices) and vegetable stew (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Anglo-Indian-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Grains — whole where practical, portioned either way", "notes": "Whole grains can help increase fibre intake. Choose them when practical; refined grains can also fit. Adjust portions to your needs rather than treating one amount as right for everyone.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable kedgeree with carrots (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with a vegetable upma, semolina with carrots and peas (1 cup) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Toor dalcha, toor dal-and-vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Ball curry, spiced mutton meatball curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a bottle-gourd sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one plate", "notes": "No single meal needs to contain every building block covered this week — protein, calcium, grains, vegetables and fruit adding up across several days is what actually matters, not any one plate ticking every box. This is the same idea this series has repeated from the start, applied one more time.", "breakfast_a": "Bread (2 slices) and vegetable stew with peas (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalcha, dal-and-vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge-gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Country captain chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge-gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a squash sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-corn curry, paneer with sweet corn and tomato (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a cucumber salad", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Looking back at this week, which building block — protein, calcium, grains, or vegetables and fruit — comes together most naturally for you, and which one takes more deliberate effort?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable kedgeree, rice-and-lentil breakfast dish (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked moong dal, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with tomato and onion, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Dalcha, dal-and-vegetable curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Country captain chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Next week looks at a nutrient with a genuinely India-specific angle: vitamin D, sunlight, and what that means in practice for your own bones and muscle function.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 125: Vitamin D — plenty of sunlight doesn't automatically mean enough of it (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   125,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Vitamin D — plenty of sunlight doesn''t automatically mean enough of it',
   '"Vitamin D supports your bones alongside calcium and plays a role in general wellbeing, but abundant sunshine where you live doesn''t guarantee your body is making enough of it — a testable, addressable gap rather than something to assume either way."',
   'Vitamin D supports your bones alongside calcium and plays a broader role in general wellbeing. Despite India''s abundant sunlight, deficiency is genuinely common — skin covering, mostly indoor routines, pollution, sunscreen use and darker skin tones all reduce how much vitamin D your skin actually makes from sun exposure. Sunshine alone cannot establish your vitamin D status one way or the other. Testing is useful in selected circumstances, but routine testing isn''t recommended for every otherwise healthy adult without a reason to test — this is different from monitoring a diagnosed deficiency or an existing supplement dose. Your clinician can advise whether testing, supplementation or monitoring makes sense for you. Food sources are also genuinely limited — fatty fish, egg yolks and fortified foods are the main ones, unlike several other nutrients this series has covered.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you have a kidney condition or a history of high blood calcium: ask your care team before starting a vitamin D supplement — your situation may call for closer monitoring or a different dose than the general guidance here. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Tripuri cooking (muya/bamboo shoot and similar preparations). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Vitamin D works alongside calcium to support your bones, and plays a broader role in general wellbeing too. Despite India''s abundant sunlight, vitamin D deficiency is common — skin covering, mostly indoor daily routines, pollution and sunscreen use all reduce how much your skin actually makes. Sunlight availability alone doesn''t establish your own status — testing is useful in selected circumstances, and your clinician can advise whether it''s worth doing for you rather than it being a routine default. Worth knowing: Use commercially prepared bamboo shoots and follow the package instructions. Do not use raw or inadequately prepared fresh shoots.", "breakfast_a": "Rice-and-vegetable porridge (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with tomato and onion, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Muya, bamboo shoot-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mustard greens sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Tripuri-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mustard greens sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why sunlight availability isn''t the whole picture", "notes": "Living somewhere sunny doesn''t automatically translate into good vitamin D status — most of your day is likely spent indoors or with skin covered, and darker skin tones need more sun exposure to make the same amount. Deliberately increasing unprotected sun exposure isn''t the preferred way to address a diagnosed low level, given the skin-cancer risk that comes with it — food or a properly guided supplement is the safer route. Keep your usual sun protection; sunscreen isn''t something to skip on the theory that it''s helping your vitamin D status.", "breakfast_a": "Muya, bamboo shoot-and-vegetable stir-fry with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stir-fry, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette with tomato and onion (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Muya, bamboo shoot-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, Tripuri-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-broccoli stir-fry, sautéed paneer with broccoli and capsicum (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Food sources are genuinely limited", "notes": "Unlike several nutrients covered elsewhere in this series, vitamin D isn''t found reliably in a wide range of everyday foods — fatty fish, egg yolks and fortified milk or cereals are the main dietary sources, and even a good diet often doesn''t fully close the gap on its own. This is one of the few nutrients where food alone commonly isn''t enough, which is part of why supplementation, guided by your own clinician, plays a bigger role here than for most other nutrients in this series — not because everyone needs routine testing, but because diet is a less complete safety net for this particular nutrient.", "breakfast_a": "Rice-and-vegetable porridge with peas (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Muya, bamboo shoot-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squash sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Tripuri-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squash sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot-and-peas sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "More isn''t automatically better here either", "notes": "Excessive vitamin D supplementation can raise blood calcium and cause harm. Do not self-prescribe high-dose treatment. Your clinician can advise on the appropriate dose and whether blood tests or monitoring are needed. Routine testing is not automatically needed for otherwise healthy adults taking ordinary recommended amounts.", "breakfast_a": "Muya, bamboo shoot-and-vegetable stir-fry with carrots and rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stir-fry, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with a vegetable idli, steamed rice-and-lentil cakes with grated carrot (3 pieces) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Tripuri-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Understanding your own supplement label", "notes": "If you take a vitamin D supplement, it''s worth knowing what''s actually on the label — the concentration (often written as IU or micrograms per drop, tablet or dose), how often you''re meant to take it, and whether any other product you''re already taking (a multivitamin, a calcium-and-vitamin-D combination) also contains vitamin D. Duplicate products stacking on top of each other, without realising it, is an easy way to end up on a higher dose than intended. If anything on the label is unclear, your pharmacist can talk you through it.", "breakfast_a": "Rice-and-vegetable porridge with carrots (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana masala, chickpea curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Tripuri-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a bottle-gourd sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one sunny afternoon", "notes": "A single sunny walk or one vitamin D-rich meal isn''t what determines your status one way or the other — it builds up gradually over time, not from any single day''s sun exposure or meal.", "breakfast_a": "Rice-and-vegetable porridge with pumpkin (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach bhurji, spiced scrambled eggs with spinach and onion (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Muya, bamboo shoot-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge-gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry, Tripuri-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge-gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a squash sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-vegetable pulao, lightly spiced rice with boiled eggs and mixed vegetables (2 eggs, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Was there anything about your own sun exposure, diet or supplement routine this week that felt worth adjusting?", "breakfast_a": "Rice-and-vegetable porridge (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with tomato and onion, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Muya, bamboo shoot-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mustard greens sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Tripuri-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mustard greens sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'Next week closes this run with a nutrient tied closely to blood clotting: vitamin K, and where it fits in your own diet.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
