-- postpartum week 126: Vitamin K — a steady everyday nutrient, and one worth mentioning if you're on blood thinners (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   126,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Vitamin K — a steady everyday nutrient, and one worth mentioning if you''re on blood thinners',
   '"Vitamin K supports your body''s normal blood clotting, drawn from both leafy greens and some fermented and animal foods — a quiet, everyday nutrient, though if you take warfarin or another vitamin K antagonist, steady, consistent intake matters more than any single meal."',
   'Vitamin K supports your body''s normal blood clotting. It comes in two dietary forms that both contribute — K1, mainly from leafy green vegetables, and K2, found in some fermented foods and animal products, though content varies and isn''t something to track separately from K1. For most people, a varied vegetable pattern across the week helps meet this need without any deliberate tracking, making it one of the more forgiving nutrients this series has covered. If you take warfarin or another vitamin K antagonist, this is genuinely worth planning around — the usual guidance is consistency in how much you eat from day to day, not avoidance. If that doesn''t apply to you, this week''s meals need no special adjustment beyond the general pattern this series already encourages.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you take warfarin or another vitamin K antagonist: keep your vitamin K intake fairly consistent from day to day rather than avoiding these foods, and talk to the clinician managing your medication before making any significant change to how much you eat. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Zeliangrong hill-tribe cooking (axone/fermented soybean, smoked meats, bamboo shoot and leafy greens). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Vitamin K plays a central role in normal blood clotting. It comes in two dietary forms — K1, mainly from leafy green vegetables, and K2, found in some fermented foods and animal products, with content that varies — and both contribute. For most people, eating a variety of vegetables through the week helps meet this need without any special planning. Worth knowing: Use commercially prepared bamboo shoots and follow the package instructions. Do not use raw or inadequately prepared fresh shoots.", "breakfast_a": "Axone-tempered vegetable stir-fry with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stir-fry, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with tomato and onion, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Mustard greens-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Smoked chicken curry, Zeliangrong-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted soybeans (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Two dietary forms, both counting toward the same role", "notes": "K1, from leafy greens like spinach, mustard greens and other vegetables, and K2, from some fermented soy foods and meats (content varies and isn''t worth tracking on its own), both support the same clotting function — you don''t need to track them separately or favour one over the other. A varied vegetable and food pattern across the week helps meet both.", "breakfast_a": "Axone-tempered vegetable stir-fry with spinach and rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stir-fry, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato omelette roll, rolled omelette with tomato and onion in a chapati (2 eggs, 1 chapati) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Mustard greens-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squash sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry, smoked and spiced, Zeliangrong-style (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squash sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "If you take warfarin or another vitamin K antagonist", "notes": "If this applies to you, consistency is the goal, not avoidance — a stable day-to-day pattern of vegetable intake is easier for your care team to manage around than swinging between very little and a lot. Worth knowing: Warfarin and other vitamin K antagonist medications work by affecting how your body uses vitamin K, so this is a genuinely different situation from general dietary advice — it''s specific to this class of medication, not anticoagulants generally. The usual guidance is not to avoid vitamin K-rich foods, but to keep your intake fairly consistent from day to day — a sudden large increase or decrease in leafy greens is more likely to affect your INR than steady, moderate intake. Talk to the clinician managing your medication before making any significant change to how much of these foods you eat.", "breakfast_a": "Rice porridge (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Zeliangrong-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a pumpkin sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-beans curry, paneer with french beans and tomato (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a beetroot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Everyday sources, without needing to plan around them", "notes": "Leafy greens, some fermented soy foods, and a well-rounded mix of vegetables through the week help most people meet their vitamin K needs without any deliberate tracking — this is one of the more forgiving nutrients in this series for anyone not on a vitamin K antagonist.", "breakfast_a": "Axone-tempered vegetable stir-fry with cabbage and rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stir-fry, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable sevai, vermicelli with mixed vegetables and scrambled egg (2 eggs, 1 cup sevai) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Chana masala, chickpea curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Rongmei-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "An easy leafy-green addition to your own meal", "notes": "You don''t need to eat leafy greens at every meal to cover your vitamin K needs, but adding a small serving somewhere in the day — a handful of spinach stirred into a curry, mustard greens as a side, or coriander and other herbs scattered over a dish — is a simple, low-effort way to build in some variety without a dedicated dish.", "breakfast_a": "Chana masala, chickpea curry with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana (1 cup curry, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Mustard greens-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge-gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Zeliangrong-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge-gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-spinach curry, boiled eggs simmered in a spiced spinach gravy (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one meal", "notes": "One especially green-heavy meal, or one day without any leafy vegetables, doesn''t meaningfully change your vitamin K status either way — it''s the overall pattern across the week, not any single plate, that matters here. If you take warfarin or another vitamin K antagonist, this general reassurance doesn''t override the day-to-day consistency your care team is tracking (see Day 3) — the pattern that matters for you is steadier and shorter than a week.", "breakfast_a": "Axone-tempered vegetable stir-fry with carrots and rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stir-fry, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Mustard greens-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans-and-carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry, Zeliangrong-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans-and-carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a squash sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-onion curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a cabbage-and-carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Looking back across this run of weeks, which single change has made the most noticeable difference to how you feel day to day?", "breakfast_a": "Axone-tempered vegetable stir-fry with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stir-fry, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with tomato and onion, spiced scrambled eggs (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Mustard greens-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Smoked chicken curry, Zeliangrong-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bamboo shoot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted soybeans (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a mixed vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'The next set of weeks continues this series'' steady, one-topic-at-a-time approach to your own nutrition and wellbeing.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 127: Thiamine (vitamin B1), easy to under-eat without noticing (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   127,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Thiamine (vitamin B1), easy to under-eat without noticing',
   '"Thiamine helps your body turn food into usable energy, drawn from whole grains, legumes, nuts and seeds — for most people eating a varied diet it''s simple to get enough, though sustained heavy alcohol use, a history of bariatric surgery, or prolonged vomiting can raise the risk of falling short."',
   'Thiamine (vitamin B1) helps your body convert carbohydrates, fats and protein into usable energy, and supports normal nerve function. It''s found across many everyday foods — whole grains, legumes, nuts and seeds, with smaller amounts in eggs and dairy — so a varied plate covers it for most people without any deliberate tracking. There''s no established Tolerable Upper Intake Level for thiamine, and no toxicity has been documented from ordinary food intake. That reflects an absence of documented harm from food, not a guarantee that any dose of supplementation is safe — high-dose thiamine supplementation still isn''t something to take without a reason. Sustained heavy alcohol use, a history of bariatric surgery, and prolonged vomiting are examples of situations that genuinely raise the risk of falling short, not an exhaustive list — each can reduce how much thiamine your body absorbs or retains. This week''s meals follow the same varied pattern this series encourages throughout.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. If you''ve had bariatric surgery: malabsorption of certain nutrients, including thiamine, is a recognised risk after some procedures — routine monitoring and supplementation guidance from your surgical or medical team should already account for this; this series'' general meal suggestions don''t replace that plan. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Sikkimese home cooking (momos, thukpa noodle soup, and fermented preparations like gundruk, sinki and kinema, drawn from Nepali, Bhutia and Lepcha communities). If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Thiamine (vitamin B1) helps your body convert the carbohydrates, fats and protein you eat into usable energy, and supports normal nerve function. It''s found across many everyday foods — whole grains, legumes, nuts and seeds, with smaller amounts in eggs and dairy — so a varied plate through the week covers it for most people without any special planning.", "breakfast_a": "Gundruk-tempered vegetable stir-fry with whole wheat roti and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stir-fry, 2 roti) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with a vegetable thukpa, noodle soup with a measured 1/2 cup cooked noodles and mixed vegetables (1 cup broth) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kinema, fermented soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked kinema) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squash sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken momos, steamed dumplings with a tomato-sesame chutney (6 pieces, approx 100g chicken filling) and a cucumber salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Whole grains carry more of it than heavily milled ones", "notes": "Whole grains — unpolished or hand-pounded rice, whole wheat, millets like kodo or finger millet — retain more thiamine than heavily milled, polished grains. You don''t need to switch what you usually eat; folding in a whole grain a few times a week is a simple way to broaden your sources.", "breakfast_a": "Kodo millet upma with vegetables and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup upma) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable thukpa, noodle soup with scrambled egg and a measured 1/2 cup cooked noodles (2 eggs, 1 cup broth) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Sinki, fermented radish-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Sikkimese-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Vegetable-and-lentil khichdi with spinach (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies)", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry, Sikkimese-style (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "A note on alcohol and absorption", "notes": "A history of bariatric surgery and a stretch of prolonged vomiting are two more examples of situations worth mentioning to your clinician for the same reason — both can lower how much thiamine your body takes in or keeps. Worth knowing: Sustained heavy alcohol use is a well-established risk factor for thiamine deficiency — it can both reduce how much your body absorbs and often coincides with lower overall food intake. If it applies to you, or you have questions about your own drinking, your clinician can help — thiamine deficiency is treatable once identified.", "breakfast_a": "Buckwheat dhido, porridge (a measured 1/2 cup cooked buckwheat, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato wrap, scrambled egg in a whole wheat roti (2 eggs, 1 roti) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kinema, fermented soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked kinema) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge-gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken thukpa, noodle soup with chicken, vegetables and a measured 1/2 cup cooked noodles (100g chicken, 1 cup broth) with a side salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a beetroot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Cooking methods that help it survive the pot", "notes": "Thiamine is water-soluble and breaks down with prolonged heat, so it''s easy to lose some in cooking. Using the cooking water in a dish rather than draining and discarding it, and not overcooking vegetables and legumes, are simple habits that help hold onto a bit more of it — nothing to track precisely.", "breakfast_a": "Gundruk-tempered vegetable stir-fry with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stir-fry, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable-and-paneer momos, steamed dumplings (6 pieces, approx 100g paneer filling) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana masala, chickpea curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry, Sikkimese-style (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "dinner_b": "Gundruk-chicken curry, fermented greens with chicken (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "An easy whole-grain swap, not a rule", "notes": "Swapping in a whole grain — millet, whole wheat, unpolished rice — for a refined one at one meal today is an easy way to add thiamine and fibre together, if it''s convenient. It isn''t a rule to follow every day, and your usual rice-based meals remain a perfectly fine base the rest of the time.", "breakfast_a": "Finger millet (ragi) roti with a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils and vegetables (2 roti, 1 cup dal-vegetable mix) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Sinki, fermented radish-and-lentil curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bottle-gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry, Sikkimese-style (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bottle-gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Rajma-and-vegetable pulao (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-spinach curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one meal", "notes": "One meal heavy in refined grains, or one day without a dedicated whole-grain dish, doesn''t meaningfully change your thiamine status — it''s the pattern of varied foods across the week, not any single plate, that matters here.", "breakfast_a": "Gundruk-tempered vegetable stir-fry with cabbage and rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stir-fry, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with capsicum (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kinema, fermented soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked kinema) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage-and-carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Sikkimese-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage-and-carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a squash sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Looking back at this week, was there a whole grain or preparation you hadn''t tried before that you''d keep making room for?", "breakfast_a": "Gundruk-tempered vegetable stir-fry with whole wheat roti and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup stir-fry, 2 roti) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with a vegetable thukpa, noodle soup with a measured 1/2 cup cooked noodles and mixed vegetables (1 cup broth) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Kinema, fermented soybean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked kinema) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squash sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken momos, steamed dumplings with a tomato-sesame chutney (6 pieces, approx 100g chicken filling) and a cucumber salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'The next set of weeks continues this series'' steady, one-topic-at-a-time approach to your own nutrition and wellbeing.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
