-- postpartum week 129: Reading a nutrition label — what's actually worth your attention (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   129,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Reading a nutrition label — what''s actually worth your attention',
   '"A nutrition label is a tool to reach for occasionally, not a test to pass at every meal — knowing where to look (serving size, the ingredient list, allergen statements) tells you more than a marketing claim on the front of the pack ever will."',
   'Reading a nutrition label well is a practical skill, not a test to pass — knowing where to look and what actually matters can make choosing between two packaged foods, or deciding whether something fits your usual pattern, a lot quicker. It''s a tool to reach for occasionally, not a habit to maintain at every meal. This week isn''t about restricting packaged food or treating it as automatically worse than home-cooked — plenty of packaged foods fit comfortably into a varied diet. The goal is simply knowing how to read what''s actually on the label, so a marketing claim on the front doesn''t do the deciding for you.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Dawoodi Bohra home cooking, a Gujarati Muslim community cuisine — dal chawal gosht, kheema, sev khatta, vegetable kharo and Bohra-style cutlets. If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "In India, check first whether the numbers on a label are given per 100 g/ml, per serving, or for the whole pack — FSSAI''s labelling rules allow information per 100 g/ml or for a single-consumption pack, and an imported product may follow a different convention again. Knowing which basis you''re looking at before comparing two products is the single most useful habit for reading a label accurately.", "breakfast_a": "Masoor dal, Bohra-style with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup dal, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with a Bohra-style chutney sandwich (2 slices bread) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable kharo, spiced vegetable stew (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Dal chawal gosht, mutton simmered with lentils (100g mutton, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted chana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rajma-and-vegetable khichdi with beans (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked beans; add water as needed, finished volume varies)", "dinner_b": "Bohra-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Ingredients are listed by weight, not by importance", "notes": "Ingredients are listed in order by weight, from most to least — glancing at the first three or four tells you what a food is mostly made of, and where added sugar sits in that list gives a sense of how prominent it is. That''s different from the quantitative added-sugar figure on the nutrition panel itself, which states the actual amount — the two tell you different things, and it''s worth checking both rather than treating one as more informative than the other. Added sugar also goes by many names (sucrose, high-fructose corn syrup, cane juice, several others ending in \"-ose\"), so recognising a few of these helps you read the ingredient list accurately alongside the figure.", "breakfast_a": "Sev khatta, tomato-and-sev curry with a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup curry) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Bohra kheema-stuffed paratha (100g minced chicken, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable kharo with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup kharo, 1 cup rice) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Bohra cutlet, spiced minced mutton patties (100g mutton, 2 cutlets) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Fish curry, Bohra-style (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Checking the allergen line, every time", "notes": "Allergen statements aren''t the only safety-relevant part of a label — storage instructions and the use-by or best-before date matter too, especially once a pack is opened. Most of the rest of this week is about making shopping a bit easier, not about avoiding packaged food. Worth knowing: Allergen statements on packaged food (\"contains,\" \"may contain traces of\") are there for a genuine safety reason, but the standard declaration doesn''t necessarily cover every allergen a person needs to avoid — if you or someone you''re cooking for has a known food allergy, checking the full ingredient list and allergen statements each time, including on a product you''ve bought before, is worth the extra few seconds. Formulations do change.", "breakfast_a": "Masoor dal, Bohra-style with spinach and rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup dal, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge-gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Dal chawal murgh, chicken simmered with lentils (100g chicken, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge-gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer Bohra curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a beetroot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "%Daily Value is a reference point, not a personal target", "notes": "Indian packaged food often shows %RDA (Recommended Dietary Allowance) rather than the %DV (Daily Value) figure used on some imported labels — the two are calculated differently and aren''t directly comparable. Either way, the percentage is based on a general reference intake, not your own individual needs — it''s a rough way to compare one food to another, not a personalised target to hit exactly at every meal.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable kharo with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup kharo, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Bohra kheema-stuffed paratha (100g minced mutton, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana masala, chickpea curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Bohra-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Toor dal khichdi with cauliflower (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked cauliflower; add water as needed, finished volume varies)", "dinner_b": "Bohra cutlet, spiced minced chicken patties (100g chicken, 2 cutlets) with roti (2 pieces) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A front-of-pack claim isn''t the whole story", "notes": "Front-of-pack claims don''t describe a food''s whole nutritional profile. Check the ingredient list and nutrition panel together, including the stated serving basis, rather than relying on the claim alone. \"No added sugar,\" for instance, has defined conditions under FSSAI rules, but meeting that definition doesn''t mean a food is sugar-free (it can still contain natural sugars) or automatically suitable for a diabetes meal plan.", "breakfast_a": "Sev khatta, tomato-and-sev curry with peas and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup curry) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable kharo with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup kharo, 1 cup rice) and a bottle-gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Dal chawal machhi, fish simmered with lentils (100g fish, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bottle-gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-spinach curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "An occasional tool, not a habit for every meal", "notes": "You don''t need to read every label on every item every time — it''s a tool to reach for occasionally, when you''re curious or deciding between two similar products, not a habit to maintain at every single meal. An important exception is managing a known food allergy, where checking the full ingredient list and allergen statements each time, on each purchase, is worth keeping up regardless — and some individual medical plans may call for regular label checking for other reasons too.", "breakfast_a": "Masoor dal, Bohra-style with carrots and rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup dal, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with capsicum (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable kharo with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup kharo, 1 cup rice) and a cabbage-and-carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Bohra-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage-and-carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a squash sabzi", "dinner_b": "Bohra-style paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Looking back at this week, is there one packaged food you buy regularly that you''d like to actually check the label on next time?", "breakfast_a": "Masoor dal, Bohra-style with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup dal, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with a Bohra-style chutney sandwich (2 slices bread) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable kharo, spiced vegetable stew (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Dal chawal gosht, mutton simmered with lentils (100g mutton, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted chana (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rajma-and-vegetable khichdi with beans (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked beans; add water as needed, finished volume varies)", "dinner_b": "Bohra-style chicken curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'The next set of weeks continues this series'' steady, one-topic-at-a-time approach to your own nutrition and wellbeing.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 130: Riboflavin (vitamin B2), a steady everyday nutrient often taken for granted (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   130,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Riboflavin (vitamin B2), a steady everyday nutrient often taken for granted',
   '"Riboflavin helps your body turn food into energy and supports normal vision and skin — found across dairy, eggs, leafy greens, almonds and mushrooms, it''s one of the easier nutrients to get enough of. A bright-yellow tinge to your urine from a supplement can be a harmless, well-known colour change, though that alone doesn''t mean every ingredient in the product is safe at any dose."',
   'Riboflavin (vitamin B2) helps your body convert food into usable energy, and supports normal vision and healthy skin. It''s found across dairy, eggs, leafy greens, almonds and mushrooms — a varied plate helps most people meet their needs without any deliberate tracking. There''s no established upper intake level for riboflavin from food, and no toxicity has been documented from ordinary intake — that reflects an absence of documented harm, not a guarantee that any dose of supplementation is safe. A diet that includes very little dairy, egg or other animal-source food — a strictly vegan diet, or one shaped by a milk allergy, lactose intolerance or another exclusion — is one recognised risk factor for falling short, and fortified plant foods and a wider mix of legumes, nuts and greens are worth leaning on if that describes you. This week''s meals follow the same varied pattern this series encourages throughout.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Saoji and Varhadi cooking (Vidarbha, eastern Maharashtra, centred on Nagpur) — fiery Saoji chicken and mutton curries alongside vegetarian Varhadi staples like zunka, vangi bharit and patodi rassa. If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Riboflavin (vitamin B2) helps your body convert food into usable energy, and supports normal vision and healthy skin. It''s found across dairy, eggs, leafy greens, almonds and mushrooms — a varied plate through the week covers it for most people without any special planning.", "breakfast_a": "Zunka, besan-based dry curry with roti and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup zunka, 2 roti) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Patodi rassa, gram-flour dumplings in a spiced gravy (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Saoji chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rajma-and-vegetable khichdi with carrots (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked carrots; add water as needed, finished volume varies)", "dinner_b": "Paneer, Saoji-style curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Everyday sources, spread across the day", "notes": "Dairy, eggs, leafy greens, almonds and mushrooms all carry riboflavin — spreading a couple of these across your day, rather than relying on just one, is an easy way to cover it without any special planning.", "breakfast_a": "Varhadi vangi bharit, roasted eggplant mash with roti and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup bharit, 2 roti) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-mushroom bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Patodi rassa with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup rassa, 1 cup rice) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Saoji mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Saoji chicken curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "A harmless, well-known supplement effect", "notes": "You don''t need to watch for this or worry about it — it''s included here only because it can be a surprising thing to notice if nobody''s mentioned it before. Worth knowing: If you take a B-complex or riboflavin supplement, a bright yellow tinge to your urine can be a harmless colour change from the riboflavin itself — riboflavin is water-soluble, and your body simply clears what it doesn''t use. That explains the colour, but it doesn''t by itself mean every ingredient in a B-complex product is safe at any dose, or that any urine colour change is automatically harmless — if you notice a change you''re unsure about, or take a high-dose product, it''s worth mentioning to your clinician or pharmacist.", "breakfast_a": "Zunka with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup zunka, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (2 eggs, 2 paratha) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Varhadi-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-beans curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a beetroot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If your diet includes very little dairy or egg", "notes": "A diet that includes very little dairy, egg or other animal-source food — including a strictly vegan diet, or one shaped by a milk allergy, lactose intolerance or another exclusion — is a genuine, worth-naming risk factor for lower riboflavin intake. Leafy greens, almonds, mushrooms, legumes and fortified plant milks or cereals are the foods actually doing the work here if that describes you; checking labels for fortification helps.", "breakfast_a": "Varhadi vangi bharit with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup bharit, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana masala, chickpea curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Saoji mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "dinner_b": "Saoji-style egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Keeping milk out of direct light, a small kitchen habit", "notes": "Riboflavin breaks down with light exposure more than with heat — keep perishable milk refrigerated as usual, and where practical choose an opaque container or keep it away from direct light too, as an addition to refrigeration rather than a substitute for it. It''s a small habit, not something to be precise about.", "breakfast_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils and vegetables (1 cup dal-vegetable mix) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Patodi rassa with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup rassa, 1 cup rice) and a bottle-gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Saoji chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bottle-gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-spinach curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one meal", "notes": "One meal without any dairy, eggs or leafy greens doesn''t meaningfully change your riboflavin status — it''s the pattern of varied foods across the week, not any single plate, that matters here.", "breakfast_a": "Zunka with rice and carrots and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup zunka, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with capsicum (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Patodi rassa with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup rassa, 1 cup rice) and a cabbage-and-carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Saoji mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage-and-carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Chana dal khichdi with squash (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana dal, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked squash; add water as needed, finished volume varies)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Looking back at this week, did knowing about the harmless urine-colour effect change how you think about a B-complex supplement you already take, if you take one?", "breakfast_a": "Zunka, besan-based dry curry with roti and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup zunka, 2 roti) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Patodi rassa, gram-flour dumplings in a spiced gravy (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Saoji chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rajma-and-vegetable khichdi with carrots (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked carrots; add water as needed, finished volume varies)", "dinner_b": "Paneer, Saoji-style curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'The next set of weeks continues this series'' steady, one-topic-at-a-time approach to your own nutrition and wellbeing.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 131: Choosing and reusing cooking oil, safely and without waste (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   131,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Choosing and reusing cooking oil, safely and without waste',
   '"The oil you cook with is part of your everyday fat intake — choosing a suitable unsaturated oil, matching it to the cooking method, and avoiding repeated reuse of frying oil are small, practical habits, not a case against fried food altogether."',
   'Choosing a suitable unsaturated cooking oil for your everyday meals, matching it to the cooking method, and storing oil sealed and away from heat and light are small, practical habits. Rotating between more than one oil across the week can add some variety if convenient, but it isn''t something you need to aim for — owning several oils isn''t a requirement. If you deep-fry, there''s one genuine safety habit worth knowing: repeatedly reheating and reusing the same batch of oil can break it down and form compounds linked to health risks with regular exposure. Avoid using the same oil for repeated frying. FSSAI allows filtered oil left over from frying to be used in curry preparation afterward, to reduce waste — that''s different from repeatedly deep-frying in the same batch. This isn''t a case against fried food — it''s specifically about not reusing the same oil for another fry.',
   'If you manage diabetes or another condition affecting blood sugar: follow your own care team''s specific guidance on portions and timing — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for carbohydrate content either. If you have PCOS: flexible, balanced eating across the week matters more than any single "PCOS diet" — there''s no special curative diet required, and the general guidance in this series applies to you too. If you take levothyroxine: follow your prescribed timing around meals, calcium-rich foods, and calcium or iron supplements — ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure about spacing. These notes are general starting points, not condition-specific menus — the meal options below aren''t matched to any particular condition''s nutrient or carbohydrate targets. Individual guidance from your own care team always takes precedence.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or you''d rather keep it simple, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite; this week''s meals are meant to be adequate, not restrictive. The "A" options are vegetarian, prepared with vegetable stock or water rather than meat or fish stock, including this week''s regional dishes. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different nutrient content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you. This week''s meals draw on Kongunadu cooking (Coimbatore and the western Tamil Nadu plains) — nattu kozhi (country chicken) curry, kola urundai (fried-then-simmered meatballs), kandarappam rice pancakes and vegetable kootu. If a regional dish is unfamiliar or unavailable, choose a familiar alternative with a similar nutritional role: dal or beans for a legume dish, a suitable protein source for a protein dish, and vegetables for a vegetable side. Keep the grain portion and any planned calcium source. These swaps are not nutritionally identical.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "The oil you cook with is part of your daily fat intake, and different oils carry different mixes of saturated, monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fat. Choosing a suitable unsaturated oil (sunflower, groundnut, mustard, rice bran, and others) for your everyday cooking matters more than which specific one you use — rotating between more than one oil across the week can add some variety if it''s convenient, but owning several oils isn''t something you need to aim for.", "breakfast_a": "Arisi paruppu sadam, rice-and-lentil dish with peas and carrots (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with kandarappam, savoury rice pancakes (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable kootu, mixed vegetable-and-lentil stew (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Nattu kozhi curry, country chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts and ellu podi, sesame powder (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rajma-and-vegetable khichdi with drumstick (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked drumstick; add water as needed, finished volume varies)", "dinner_b": "Kola urundai, fried-then-simmered meatball curry (100g minced chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Matching the oil to the heat", "notes": "Different oils, and different levels of refinement of the same oil, have different smoke points — the temperature at which they start to break down and smoke — so oil type and refinement both matter; the same name (like olive oil) can behave quite differently depending on how refined it is. Rather than memorising specific temperatures, the simpler habit is: choose an oil suited to the cooking method you''re using, follow the label''s guidance if it gives one, and avoid heating any oil until it''s smoking.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi kali, finger millet porridge with spinach (a measured 1/2 cup cooked ragi, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion kandarappam, savoury rice pancakes with egg (2 eggs, 2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable kootu with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup kootu, 1 cup rice) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Kola urundai, fried-then-simmered meatball curry (100g minced mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut papaya", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted cashews (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Nattu kozhi curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Reusing frying oil, safely", "notes": "This isn''t about avoiding fried food altogether — it''s a specific habit around how many times the same batch of oil gets reused. Worth knowing: Repeatedly reheating and reusing the same oil for deep frying, especially at high temperatures, can break it down and form compounds linked to health risks with regular exposure. Avoid using the same oil for repeated frying. FSSAI allows filtered oil left over from a fry to be used in curry preparation afterward, to reduce waste — that''s different from repeatedly deep-frying in the same batch, which is the habit to avoid. If you keep filtered oil for curry preparation, store it separately from fresh oil and use it within one or two days. Discard oil immediately, rather than reusing it, if it''s darkened, thickened, foams up, smells off, or starts smoking at a lower temperature than before.", "breakfast_a": "Arisi paruppu sadam with beans (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma, kidney bean curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge-gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Kongunadu-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a ridge-gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a guava", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted almonds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-beans curry (100g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a beetroot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Ordinary, well-stored oil works just as well", "notes": "An ordinary, everyday cooking oil stored properly — sealed, away from direct heat and light — holds up just as well as an expensive specialty oil for most home cooking. You don''t need anything exotic for this week''s meals to work.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable kootu with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup kootu, 1 cup rice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices bread) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Chana masala, chickpea curry (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked chana) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Nattu kozhi curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a pear", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Toor dal-and-vegetable khichdi with beans (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked toor dal, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked beans; add water as needed, finished volume varies)", "dinner_b": "Chicken chettinad, Kongu-style pepper chicken curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "An easy oil swap, not a rule", "notes": "Swapping in a different oil than usual for one dish this week — if you happen to have more than one at home — is a simple way to add variety. It isn''t a rule to follow every day, and your usual oil the rest of the week is a perfectly fine choice.", "breakfast_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils and vegetables (1 cup dal-vegetable mix) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable kootu with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup kootu, 1 cup rice) and a bottle-gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Nattu kozhi curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bottle-gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a cup of cut watermelon", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg-and-spinach curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The pattern across your week matters, not one dish", "notes": "One meal that''s fried rather than steamed or simmered doesn''t meaningfully change things on its own — enjoying fried food occasionally is completely fine. Keep that separate from the oil-reuse habit from earlier this week, though: avoid using the same oil for another round of frying. Filtered leftover oil going into a curry instead is fine and reduces waste — that''s a different thing from reusing it to deep-fry again.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi kali with cabbage (a measured 1/2 cup cooked ragi, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with capsicum (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable kootu with rice and a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils (1 cup kootu, 1 cup rice) and a cabbage-and-carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry, Kongunadu-style (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage-and-carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with an orange", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a squash sabzi", "dinner_b": "Muttai masala, spiced egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Looking back at this week, is there a habit around your frying oil — how you store it, how many times you reuse it — you''d like to change?", "breakfast_a": "Arisi paruppu sadam, rice-and-lentil dish with peas and carrots (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked vegetables; add water as needed, finished volume varies) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with kandarappam, savoury rice pancakes (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable kootu, mixed vegetable-and-lentil stew (1 cup, includes a measured 1/2 cup cooked lentils) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Nattu kozhi curry, country chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted peanuts and ellu podi, sesame powder (15g) and a piece of seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Rajma-and-vegetable khichdi with drumstick (a measured 1/2 cup cooked rice, a measured 1/2 cup cooked rajma, and a separately measured 1/2 cup cooked drumstick; add water as needed, finished volume varies)", "dinner_b": "Kola urundai, fried-then-simmered meatball curry (100g minced chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small habit from this week — a food, a routine — that felt worth keeping, without needing to overhaul everything at once?',
   'The next set of weeks continues this series'' steady, one-topic-at-a-time approach to your own nutrition and wellbeing.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;
